00:57:38,490 --> 00:57:40,858
They say he made a good end.

1488
00:57:40,892 --> 00:57:42,560
And will he not come again?

1489
00:57:42,593 --> 00:57:44,962
MIKE:
Uh-huh. Hey,
what do you have in a rye?

1490
00:57:44,995 --> 00:57:47,698
- And will he not come again?

1491
00:57:47,732 --> 00:57:49,500
SERVO:
So, you didn't take
your Zoloft, huh?

1492
00:57:49,533 --> 00:57:52,037
[CHUCKLING]
He is gone.

1493
00:57:52,070 --> 00:57:54,805
He is gone
and we cast away moan.

1494
00:57:54,838 --> 00:57:58,108
God have mercy on his soul
and of all Christian souls,

1495
00:57:58,142 --> 00:57:59,343
I pray God.

1496
00:57:59,377 --> 00:58:00,978
God be with you.

1497
00:58:01,012 --> 00:58:02,413
[HUMMING]

1498
00:58:02,447 --> 00:58:03,715
- Do you see this?

1499
00:58:03,748 --> 00:58:05,450
Oh God.

1500
00:58:05,483 --> 00:58:08,052
MIKE:
I'm going over
to Lady MacBeth's.

1501
00:58:08,085 --> 00:58:11,923
- Laertes, I must commune
with your grief...

1502
00:58:11,957 --> 00:58:13,357
SERVO:
Would you stop
rubbing my nipples?

1503
00:58:13,390 --> 00:58:14,958
- ...or you deny me right.

1504
00:58:15,025 --> 00:58:16,962
Make choice from
your wisest friends you will

1505
00:58:16,962 --> 00:58:19,097
and they shall judge
twixt you and me...

1506
00:58:19,097 --> 00:58:20,465
CROW:
Don't get a boner.

1507
00:58:20,498 --> 00:58:25,168
- ...and where the offence is,
let the great axe fall.

1508
00:58:25,203 --> 00:58:26,603
MIKE:
So we're going to chop wood.

1509
00:58:26,637 --> 00:58:28,505
Do you have to be so dramatic?

1510
00:58:30,275 --> 00:58:31,877
- There's a letter
for you, sir.

1511
00:58:31,910 --> 00:58:34,212
It came from the ambassador
that was bound for England.

1512
00:58:34,245 --> 00:58:36,480
MIKE:
Um, there's postage due.

1513
00:58:38,983 --> 00:58:41,418
SERVO:
Ophelia invoiced me
for the wheat!

1514
00:58:42,420 --> 00:58:44,055
- 'Ere we were
two days old at sea,

1515
00:58:44,088 --> 00:58:45,957
a pirate
of very warlike appointment

1516
00:58:45,990 --> 00:58:46,958
gave us chase.

1517
00:58:46,992 --> 00:58:48,660
Finding ourselves
too slow of sail,

1518
00:58:48,693 --> 00:58:50,828
we put on compelled valor
and in the grapple

1519
00:58:50,862 --> 00:58:54,265
I boarded them on the instant
they got clear of our ship

1520
00:58:54,298 --> 00:58:56,533
so I alone
became their prisoner.

1521
00:58:56,567 --> 00:58:58,368
CROW:
Hamlet faxed me a soliloquy.

1522
00:58:58,402 --> 00:59:01,305
- They have dealt
with my like thieves of mercy,

1523
00:59:01,338 --> 00:59:02,940
but they knew what they did.

1524
00:59:02,973 --> 00:59:04,575
Let the king
have the letters I have sent

1525
00:59:04,608 --> 00:59:08,545
and repair thou to me as quickly
as thou wouldst fly death.

1526
00:59:08,580 --> 00:59:10,348
These good fellows...

1527
00:59:10,382 --> 00:59:11,917
MIKE:
Hmm, I don't know about that.

1528
00:59:11,950 --> 00:59:13,417
- ...will bring thee
where I am.

1529
00:59:13,450 --> 00:59:15,052
I have words to speak
in thine ear

1530
00:59:15,085 --> 00:59:16,955
will make thee dumb.
Farewell.

1531
00:59:16,988 --> 00:59:20,057
He that thou knowest thine,
Hamlet.

1532
00:59:20,090 --> 00:59:22,193
SERVO:
I could crap a better letter
than this.

1533
00:59:22,227 --> 00:59:24,895
[♪♪♪]

1534
00:59:28,565 --> 00:59:30,868
MIKE:
Oh, man!
I just played handball.

1535
00:59:30,902 --> 00:59:31,902
Oh!

1536
00:59:34,138 --> 00:59:36,107
CROW:
My contacts are fine.

1537
00:59:36,140 --> 00:59:37,875
- Laertes...

1538
00:59:39,810 --> 00:59:42,180
your sister is drowned.

1539
00:59:43,513 --> 00:59:45,650
- Drowned, you say?

1540
00:59:45,683 --> 00:59:46,650
Where?

1541
00:59:46,683 --> 00:59:47,652
SERVO:
Well, she jumped in a lake

1542
00:59:47,685 --> 00:59:48,687
trying to save some wheat.

1543
00:59:48,720 --> 00:59:52,757
- There is a willow
grows aslant a brook

1544
00:59:52,790 --> 00:59:55,993
that shows his hoar leaves
in the glassy stream.

1545
00:59:56,027 --> 01:00:01,898
There did she come
with fantastic garlands...

1546
01:00:01,932 --> 01:00:04,068
MIKE:
So, the creek?

1547
01:00:07,938 --> 01:00:09,973
MIKE:
Hey! Hey, do I rock
the Slurpee machine

1548
01:00:10,007 --> 01:00:11,608
when you're trying to work?

1549
01:00:11,642 --> 01:00:16,013
- How long will a man lie
in the earth ere he rot?

1550
01:00:16,047 --> 01:00:18,650
- Sir, if he be not rotten
before he die,

1551
01:00:18,683 --> 01:00:21,718
he will last you some
eight or nine year.

1552
01:00:21,752 --> 01:00:22,720
SERVO:
Bleh bleh bleh bleh bleh...

1553
01:00:22,753 --> 01:00:24,755
- A tanner will
last you nine year.

1554
01:00:24,788 --> 01:00:27,158
- Why he more than another?

1555
01:00:27,192 --> 01:00:29,793
- Why sir, his hide
is so tanned with his trade

1556
01:00:29,827 --> 01:00:33,430
that he will keep out water
a great while...

1557
01:00:33,463 --> 01:00:35,198
...and your water
is a sore decayer

1558
01:00:35,232 --> 01:00:37,368
of your whoreson dead body.

1559
01:00:37,402 --> 01:00:38,602
Here's a skull...

1560
01:00:38,635 --> 01:00:40,472
CROW:
Not a bad skull.
Good starter skull.

1561
01:00:40,505 --> 01:00:43,273
- ...now has lain in the earth
three and twenty years.

1562
01:00:43,307 --> 01:00:44,275
SERVO:
Bleh bleh bleh...

1563
01:00:44,308 --> 01:00:45,777
- Whose was it?

1564
01:00:45,810 --> 01:00:48,478
- A pestilence on him
for a mad rogue!

1565
01:00:48,512 --> 01:00:49,547
CROW:
Why I oughtta...!

1566
01:00:49,580 --> 01:00:51,415
- Poured a flagon
of Rhenish on my head once.

1567
01:00:51,448 --> 01:00:53,117
[CHUCKLING]
[MIKE CHUCKLING]

1568
01:00:53,150 --> 01:00:56,053
- This same skull, sir,
was Sir Yorick's skull,

1569
01:00:56,087 --> 01:00:57,522
the king's jester.

1570
01:00:57,555 --> 01:00:58,723
- This?

1571
01:00:58,757 --> 01:01:00,257
MIKE:
I just saw him
at the Chuckle Hut.

1572
01:01:00,257 --> 01:01:02,127
- Aye, aye.

1573
01:01:02,193 --> 01:01:03,127
E'en that.

1574
01:01:03,193 --> 01:01:04,628
- Let me see.

1575
01:01:04,662 --> 01:01:06,597
CROW:
Mmm! Nice zygomatic arch.

1576
01:01:09,267 --> 01:01:10,233
SERVO:
I remember him

1577
01:01:10,267 --> 01:01:11,735
in his big, pointy shoes,
stupid hat.

1578
01:01:11,768 --> 01:01:14,505
He had the worst act, I swear.

1579
01:01:14,538 --> 01:01:16,007
- Alas, poor Yorick.

1580
01:01:16,040 --> 01:01:18,843
MIKE:
Hey, I did alright for myself.

1581
01:01:18,877 --> 01:01:20,545
- I knew him, Horatio.

1582
01:01:20,578 --> 01:01:22,380
CROW:
I sat next to him in Trig.

1583
01:01:22,413 --> 01:01:25,215
- A fellow of infinite jest...

1584
01:01:25,248 --> 01:01:27,185
...of most excellent fancy.

1585
01:01:27,218 --> 01:01:30,353
He hath borne me on his back
a thousand times.

1586
01:01:30,388 --> 01:01:31,722
SERVO:
'Course I bored him a lot too.

1587
01:01:31,755 --> 01:01:32,823
Heh heh.

1588
01:01:32,857 --> 01:01:36,127
- And now how abhorred
in my imagination it is.

1589
01:01:36,160 --> 01:01:37,128
MIKE:
Wait.

1590
01:01:37,162 --> 01:01:39,130
This isn't Yorick,
this is George Gobel.

1591
01:01:39,163 --> 01:01:41,132
- Here hung those lips
that I have kissed

1592
01:01:41,165 --> 01:01:42,233
I know not how oft.

1593
01:01:42,267 --> 01:01:43,233
CROW:
You're a little cutie pie skull,

1594
01:01:43,267 --> 01:01:44,935
aren't you,
with your button nose?

1595
01:01:44,968 --> 01:01:45,937
You little cutie skull.

1596
01:01:45,970 --> 01:01:48,072
- Where be your jibes now?

1597
01:01:48,105 --> 01:01:49,073
You gamboled...

1598
01:01:49,107 --> 01:01:50,508
SERVO:
So they banned you from jesting.

1599
01:01:50,542 --> 01:01:52,543
- Your songs?

1600
01:01:52,577 --> 01:01:54,445
Your flashes of merriment

1601
01:01:54,478 --> 01:01:58,315
that were wont
to set the table on a roar?

1602
01:01:58,348 --> 01:02:01,618
Not one now
to mock your own grinning.

1603
01:02:01,652 --> 01:02:03,553
MIKE:
Well, there was that one mouth
that mocked it.

1604
01:02:03,587 --> 01:02:05,757
- Quite chap fallen.

1605
01:02:07,225 --> 01:02:10,528
Now get you
to my lady's chamber

1606
01:02:10,562 --> 01:02:15,800
and tell her
let her paint an inch thick,

1607
01:02:15,833 --> 01:02:18,702
to this favor she must come.

1608
01:02:18,735 --> 01:02:20,303
Go.

1609
01:02:20,337 --> 01:02:21,738
Make her laugh at that.

1610
01:02:21,772 --> 01:02:22,907
CROW:
Uh, please speak up.

1611
01:02:22,907 --> 01:02:24,942
My tympanic membrane
rotted away some years ago.

1612
01:02:24,942 --> 01:02:27,077
- Horatio, tell me one thing.

1613
01:02:28,378 --> 01:02:29,980
Dost thou think Alexander

1614
01:02:30,013 --> 01:02:31,715
looked 'o this fashion
in the earth?

1615
01:02:31,748 --> 01:02:32,717
SERVO:
I don't know, man.

1616
01:02:32,750 --> 01:02:34,018
Let's go to the tavern.

1617
01:02:34,052 --> 01:02:36,187
- E'en so.

1618
01:02:36,220 --> 01:02:37,622
- And smelled so?

1619
01:02:37,655 --> 01:02:39,123
MIKE:
Always did too. Huh!

1620
01:02:39,157 --> 01:02:40,958
- E'en so.

1621
01:02:40,992 --> 01:02:42,860
CROW:
Ah, forget it. I never knew him.

1622
01:02:42,893 --> 01:02:44,662
I made it all up.

1623
01:02:44,695 --> 01:02:47,765
- To what base uses
we return, Horatio.

1624
01:02:47,798 --> 01:02:49,233
SERVO:
My god, that's not dirt!

1625
01:02:50,267 --> 01:02:51,568
- Why may not imagination

1626
01:02:51,602 --> 01:02:53,303
trace the noble dust
of Alexander

1627
01:02:53,337 --> 01:02:55,840
'til he find it
stopping a bung-hole?

1628
01:02:55,873 --> 01:02:57,975
MIKE: Dude!
CROW: Right.

1629
01:02:58,008 --> 01:03:00,878
- 'Twere to consider too
curiously to consider so.

1630
01:03:00,912 --> 01:03:03,513
- No. No.

1631
01:03:03,547 --> 01:03:06,150
Alexander died,
Alexander was buried,

1632
01:03:06,183 --> 01:03:08,887
Alexander returneth to dust,

1633
01:03:08,920 --> 01:03:11,888
dust is earth,
of earth we make loam...

1634
01:03:11,922 --> 01:03:13,057
SERVO:
Tch! Yeah.

1635
01:03:13,090 --> 01:03:14,792
- ...and why of that loam,
whereto he was converted,

1636
01:03:14,825 --> 01:03:17,328
might they not stop
a beer barrel?

1637
01:03:17,362 --> 01:03:18,328
[BELL RINGING]

1638
01:03:18,362 --> 01:03:19,430
CROW:
It's the ice cream man!

1639
01:03:21,632 --> 01:03:22,600
SERVO:
Here comes my cow.

1640
01:03:22,633 --> 01:03:24,002
I gotta go.

1641
01:03:39,050 --> 01:03:40,050
[APPLAUSE]

1642
01:03:40,083 --> 01:03:43,553
- Hello and welcome
to "Alas Poor Who?"

1643
01:03:43,587 --> 01:03:44,722
Now as you know,

1644
01:03:44,755 --> 01:03:46,823
last week our reigning
champion, Mister Tom Servo,

1645
01:03:46,890 --> 01:03:49,660
won the fortnight's supply
of mutton

1646
01:03:49,693 --> 01:03:52,297
and he's back this week
to face another challenger.

1647
01:03:52,330 --> 01:03:53,530
[APPLAUSE]

1648
01:03:53,563 --> 01:03:54,865
- Thanks, Mike.
Thanks, everyone.

1649
01:03:54,898 --> 01:03:57,302
- Tom, remind the folks at home
what you do again.

1650
01:03:57,335 --> 01:04:00,572
- Mike, I am a small robot
who lives with you.

1651
01:04:00,605 --> 01:04:02,507
- Robot who lives with me.
That's interesting.

1652
01:04:02,540 --> 01:04:05,743
Okay, and your challenger,
Crow T. Robot.

1653
01:04:05,777 --> 01:04:06,743
How are you, sir?

1654
01:04:06,777 --> 01:04:08,112
- Ah, feelin' good, Mike.
Thanks.

1655
01:04:08,145 --> 01:04:09,513
- Mmm-hmm,
and what do you do, sir?

1656
01:04:09,547 --> 01:04:12,417
- I am also a small robot
who lives with you.

1657
01:04:12,450 --> 01:04:15,152
- Wow. Seems to be
a popular job these days.

1658
01:04:15,185 --> 01:04:16,587
[ALL LAUGHING]

1659
01:04:16,620 --> 01:04:18,855
[APPLAUSE]

1660
01:04:18,890 --> 01:04:20,023
- Let's get down to action.

1661
01:04:20,057 --> 01:04:22,493
Okay, for you folks at home,
the object of the game

1662
01:04:22,527 --> 01:04:24,662
is to visually identify
a person

1663
01:04:24,695 --> 01:04:28,465
by some part
of his or her skeletal system,

1664
01:04:28,498 --> 01:04:33,803
and Servo, you'll be starting
with this lovely femur.

1665
01:04:33,837 --> 01:04:35,573
Okay, you have ten seconds.
Go.

1666
01:04:35,607 --> 01:04:37,808
- Let's see.
Alas, poor Larry Hagman?

1667
01:04:37,842 --> 01:04:41,112
Oh, no, no.
Alas, poor Katherine Graham.

1668
01:04:41,145 --> 01:04:42,580
No.
Alas, poor... oh, shoot.

1669
01:04:42,613 --> 01:04:45,048
Oh, I got it!
Alas, poor Biz Markie.

1670
01:04:45,082 --> 01:04:46,050
[DINGING]

1671
01:04:46,083 --> 01:04:47,050
- Exactly!
Biz Markie!

1672
01:04:47,083 --> 01:04:48,318
[APPLAUSE]

1673
01:04:48,352 --> 01:04:49,553
This is his femur.

1674
01:04:49,587 --> 01:04:51,422
Surprisingly delicate,
isn't it?

1675
01:04:51,455 --> 01:04:52,657
[ALL LAUGHING]
Alright, well played.

1676
01:04:52,690 --> 01:04:54,058
Now, Crow,
you ready to give it a whirl?

1677
01:04:54,092 --> 01:04:55,292
- Oh, I sure am, Mike.

1678
01:04:55,325 --> 01:04:56,627
- Okay, then.

1679
01:04:56,660 --> 01:04:59,230
Here's a clavicle.

1680
01:04:59,263 --> 01:05:00,563
Whose is it?

1681
01:05:00,598 --> 01:05:03,233
- Wow. I wanna say Tim Conway,
but no,

1682
01:05:03,267 --> 01:05:05,268
more like Doctor Dean Ornish.

1683
01:05:05,302 --> 01:05:08,172
Uh, Germaine Greer,
Peter Cetera.

1684
01:05:08,238 --> 01:05:09,173
Geez. No, no!

1685
01:05:09,207 --> 01:05:11,508
I'm gonna go with
Nancy Allen, Mike.

1686
01:05:11,542 --> 01:05:13,510
- Please state it in
"Alas Poor" form.

1687
01:05:13,543 --> 01:05:16,447
- Oh, yeah.
Alas, poor Nancy Allen.

1688
01:05:16,480 --> 01:05:17,448
- Is right!

1689
01:05:17,482 --> 01:05:18,448
[DINGING]
- Yeah!

1690
01:05:18,482 --> 01:05:21,018
- That's a fine clavicle
she has there.

1691
01:05:21,052 --> 01:05:22,587
Must have drank lots of milk.

1692
01:05:22,620 --> 01:05:24,388
[ALL LAUGHING]

1693
01:05:24,422 --> 01:05:25,690
- Okay, it's all tied up.

1694
01:05:25,723 --> 01:05:30,427
Servo,
who belongs to this ileum?

1695
01:05:30,460 --> 01:05:31,428
- Oh, that's easy, Mike.

1696
01:05:31,462 --> 01:05:34,132
Alas, poor Alaska Senator
Ted Stevens.

1697
01:05:34,165 --> 01:05:35,365
Oh, oh, wait, no!

1698
01:05:35,398 --> 01:05:37,335
I'm sorry.
Alas, poor Roland Gift.

1699
01:05:37,368 --> 01:05:38,335
Yeah, yeah.

1700
01:05:38,368 --> 01:05:41,138
I'm gonna go with
"Alas, poor Roland Gift."

1701
01:05:41,172 --> 01:05:43,373
[BUZZING]
- Ooh. No, I'm sorry.

1702
01:05:43,407 --> 01:05:46,543
Alas, poor Alaska Senator
Ted Stevens.

1703
01:05:46,577 --> 01:05:47,645
- Oh, darn.

1704
01:05:47,678 --> 01:05:48,945
[CHUCKLING]
- Close though.

1705
01:05:48,980 --> 01:05:51,815
Okay, Crow, now you
could win with this one so...

1706
01:05:51,848 --> 01:05:52,850
watch carefully.

1707
01:05:52,883 --> 01:05:57,053
Whose are these series
of metatarsal bones?

1708
01:05:57,087 --> 01:05:58,155
- Ooh! Ooh!
I know this one.

1709
01:05:58,188 --> 01:06:00,658
Easy.
Ralph Waite.

1710
01:06:00,692 --> 01:06:03,093
- They are indeed the
metatarsals of Ralph Waite...

1711
01:06:03,127 --> 01:06:04,495
- Yay!

1712
01:06:04,528 --> 01:06:06,463
- ...but, alas, incorrect form
for the second time...

1713
01:06:06,497 --> 01:06:07,465
[BUZZING]

1714
01:06:07,498 --> 01:06:08,465
...so you don't get the points

1715
01:06:08,498 --> 01:06:10,668
and so nobody wins
and you both lose...

1716
01:06:10,702 --> 01:06:12,670
...and that's all the time
we have. Ha ha!

1717
01:06:12,703 --> 01:06:13,737
[APPLAUSE]

1718
01:06:13,770 --> 01:06:14,972
We'll be back next week
with another edition

1719
01:06:15,005 --> 01:06:16,573
of "Alas, Poor Who?"

1720
01:06:16,607 --> 01:06:18,342
[APPLAUSE]

1721
01:06:18,375 --> 01:06:19,543
- Uh, Mike we won't really
be back

1722
01:06:19,577 --> 01:06:21,178
next week, will we?

1723
01:06:21,212 --> 01:06:22,880
- Not unless I get a codpiece.

1724
01:06:22,913 --> 01:06:24,182
[CHUCKLING]

1725
01:06:24,215 --> 01:06:27,217
[♪♪♪]

1726
01:06:30,487 --> 01:06:33,457
[BELLS TOLLING]

1727
01:06:33,490 --> 01:06:37,462
MIKE:
Ah! Saint Olaf's Day parade
is pretty dour this year.

1728
01:06:40,430 --> 01:06:41,598
SERVO:
