01:27:31,347 --> 01:27:34,183
Filthy misogynist Paul Verhoeven
played the gravedigger.

2223
01:27:34,217 --> 01:27:35,285
What do you know?

2224
01:27:37,220 --> 01:27:41,023
SERVO:
♪ Doo doo doo doo
Doo doo doo doo doo ♪

2225
01:27:41,057 --> 01:27:46,228
CROW:
With Adolf
Schlyssenshyssenseddleschly

2226
01:27:46,262 --> 01:27:48,597
SERVO:
...der.

2227
01:27:48,632 --> 01:27:50,500
MIKE:
Danish consultant, Victor Borge.

2228
01:27:50,533 --> 01:27:52,068
SERVO:
Sardines courtesy King Oscar.

2229
01:27:52,102 --> 01:27:53,870
Look for special
"Hamlet" promotional cans.

2230
01:27:53,903 --> 01:27:54,870
CROW:
Also try Rosencrantz

2231
01:27:54,903 --> 01:27:56,372
and Guildenstern kipper snacks,

2232
01:27:56,405 --> 01:27:59,042
and Laertes
packed in heavy oil.

2233
01:27:59,075 --> 01:28:01,343
[♪♪♪]

2234
01:28:01,377 --> 01:28:02,345
MIKE:
Hey, I tell you,

2235
01:28:02,378 --> 01:28:03,880
between this movie
and the heating system

2236
01:28:03,913 --> 01:28:04,880
on the Volkswagen...

2237
01:28:04,913 --> 01:28:05,882
CROW:
Tch! Ick.

2238
01:28:05,915 --> 01:28:07,650
Gottschalk!

2239
01:28:09,885 --> 01:28:12,222
[SERVO HUMMING]

2240
01:28:12,255 --> 01:28:13,957
MIKE:
Thanks, Franz.

2241
01:28:13,990 --> 01:28:16,692
CROW:
Oh, yeah,
this was munchen all right. Huh!

2242
01:28:17,727 --> 01:28:19,228
MIKE:
Nice play, Shakespeare.

2243
01:28:19,262 --> 01:28:22,365
[GEARS GRINDING]

2244
01:28:32,575 --> 01:28:35,712
- So, you guys made
a Hamlet action figure, huh?

2245
01:28:35,745 --> 01:28:37,380
- Yup.
- Uh-huh.

2246
01:28:37,413 --> 01:28:39,082
- Anything remarkable about it?

2247
01:28:39,115 --> 01:28:40,417
Typical action figure?

2248
01:28:40,450 --> 01:28:43,052
You just kind of move it around,
that's the action?

2249
01:28:43,085 --> 01:28:44,753
- Yeah, that's about it.
- Pretty much.

2250
01:28:44,787 --> 01:28:46,055
- Well, you might want to
pull the string there.

2251
01:28:46,088 --> 01:28:47,057
- Oh, yeah.
- That's kind of nice.

2252
01:28:47,090 --> 01:28:48,623
- Oh, it talks.
- Uh-huh.

2253
01:28:48,657 --> 01:28:49,658
- Well, say.
Well, wow.

2254
01:28:52,062 --> 01:28:53,295
Kind of a long string.

2255
01:28:53,328 --> 01:28:55,163
- Well, that's what makes it
such a great action figure.

2256
01:28:55,197 --> 01:28:56,265
- Yeah.

2257
01:28:56,298 --> 01:28:58,333
- I know, but most action
figures with a string,

2258
01:28:58,367 --> 01:29:00,403
you know, you pull it,
it's like 18 inches long.

2259
01:29:00,437 --> 01:29:02,272
- It is a bit longer
on this one, I grant you.

2260
01:29:02,305 --> 01:29:03,472
- Mmm-hmm.

2261
01:29:03,505 --> 01:29:05,775
- Yeah, I know, but I mean,
it's really a lot longer.

2262
01:29:05,808 --> 01:29:09,212
- Well, Hamlet does have
some long speeches, Mike.

2263
01:29:09,245 --> 01:29:10,312
Oh, watch the umbrella stand.

2264
01:29:10,345 --> 01:29:11,313
- Ow! Ooh!

2265
01:29:11,347 --> 01:29:12,315
- Okay, keep going, honey.

2266
01:29:12,348 --> 01:29:13,717
It can't be too much more.

2267
01:29:13,750 --> 01:29:16,118
See, Mike, what I'd do
is go up those stairs there.

2268
01:29:16,152 --> 01:29:18,287
- Up the stairs, right.
- No, no, behind that.

2269
01:29:18,320 --> 01:29:21,190
MIKE:
I didn't even know
we had stairs here.

2270
01:29:21,223 --> 01:29:23,058
Never been over to this part
of the ship.

2271
01:29:23,092 --> 01:29:24,060
- Heh-heh.

2272
01:29:24,093 --> 01:29:25,227
MIKE:
Hey, how much longer is it?

2273
01:29:25,260 --> 01:29:27,397
- Not much!
Uh-oh. Uh-oh.

2274
01:29:27,430 --> 01:29:28,497
Pearl's calling.

2275
01:29:28,530 --> 01:29:29,498
MIKE:
I didn't know there was

2276
01:29:29,532 --> 01:29:30,567
a squash court up here.

2277
01:29:30,600 --> 01:29:33,068
- Yes, Pearl?
- Keep going.

2278
01:29:33,102 --> 01:29:35,003
- Look, I don't care
if you're small but pivotal.

2279
01:29:35,037 --> 01:29:37,840
You just can't waltz in here
and... hang on.

2280
01:29:37,873 --> 01:29:38,842
Hi, guys.

2281
01:29:38,875 --> 01:29:40,643
Got a little bit
of a situation here.

2282
01:29:40,677 --> 01:29:42,378
Apparently in Hamlet,
there's a character

2283
01:29:42,412 --> 01:29:43,378
named Fountainbras...

2284
01:29:43,412 --> 01:29:44,913
- Ah, it's Fortinbras.
- Yes.

2285
01:29:44,947 --> 01:29:45,915
- That's what I said.

2286
01:29:45,948 --> 01:29:48,083
- No, no, no,
actually you said...

2287
01:29:48,117 --> 01:29:49,252
Fortinbras.

2288
01:29:49,285 --> 01:29:52,488
- So, anyway he claims
he's in the original Hamlet

2289
01:29:52,555 --> 01:29:53,722
but he's not in this movie...

2290
01:29:53,757 --> 01:29:54,723
- Yes, and I'm not leaving

2291
01:29:54,757 --> 01:29:56,025
until I'm allowed
to play my role.

2292
01:29:56,058 --> 01:29:58,060
Am I simply
to hang about in limbo?

2293
01:29:58,093 --> 01:30:01,363
Or perhaps I should force myself
on another play, hmm?

2294
01:30:01,397 --> 01:30:03,432
Walk up to American Buffalo
and say,

2295
01:30:03,465 --> 01:30:06,302
"Hello, have you a need
for a Norwegian nobleman

2296
01:30:06,335 --> 01:30:08,737
to arrange for bodies
to be carted away?"

2297
01:30:08,772 --> 01:30:11,307
I don't think
I'm being unreasonable here.

2298
01:30:11,340 --> 01:30:13,308
- Play the part.

2299
01:30:13,342 --> 01:30:14,710
- Really?
- Sure. Go ahead.

2300
01:30:14,743 --> 01:30:15,978
Play the part.

2301
01:30:16,012 --> 01:30:20,282
- Well, I'll need
two English ambassadors.

2302
01:30:20,315 --> 01:30:21,350
- Will you?

2303
01:30:21,383 --> 01:30:23,752
- Yes, and the Horatio
and a band of soldiers

2304
01:30:23,787 --> 01:30:25,588
and drums at the beginning
and at the end

2305
01:30:25,622 --> 01:30:28,657
and a peal of ordnance
and, of course, a body.

2306
01:30:28,690 --> 01:30:30,760
- Oh, golly.

2307
01:30:30,793 --> 01:30:36,265
Where can I find a body
on such short notice? Hmm.

2308
01:30:36,298 --> 01:30:37,267
I wonder where.

2309
01:30:37,300 --> 01:30:38,935
Say, could you pour
this in your ear

2310
01:30:38,968 --> 01:30:40,002
for me for just a minute?

2311
01:30:40,035 --> 01:30:41,903
- Certainly.
- A body, a body...

2312
01:30:41,937 --> 01:30:44,773
[CHOKING]

2313
01:30:44,807 --> 01:30:46,008
Well, hey!

2314
01:30:46,042 --> 01:30:47,210
There's a body.

2315
01:30:47,243 --> 01:30:48,812
You're in luck.

2316
01:30:48,845 --> 01:30:49,812
Is it me?

2317
01:30:49,845 --> 01:30:51,880
Am I a magnet
for these idiots?

2318
01:30:51,913 --> 01:30:53,415
- He made me look butch.

2319
01:30:54,883 --> 01:30:56,952
SERVO:
Okay, let 'er go, Mike.

2320
01:30:56,985 --> 01:30:58,020
[♪♪♪]

2321
01:30:58,053 --> 01:31:03,960
- To be, or not to be:
that is the question.

2322
01:31:03,993 --> 01:31:06,695
Whether 'tis nobler in the mind

2323
01:31:06,728 --> 01:31:12,268
to suffer the slings and arrows
of outrageous fortune,

2324
01:31:12,268 --> 01:31:16,038
or to take arms
against a sea of troubles,

2325
01:31:16,038 --> 01:31:18,773
and by opposing end them?

2326
01:31:21,210 --> 01:31:24,247
To die; to sleep;

2327
01:31:26,215 --> 01:31:27,350
no more;

2328
01:31:28,750 --> 01:31:32,087
and by a sleep
to say we end the heart-ache

2329
01:31:32,120 --> 01:31:36,125
and the thousand natural shocks
that flesh is heir to,

2330
01:31:36,158 --> 01:31:39,628
'tis a consummation devoutly
to be wish'd.

2331
01:31:39,662 --> 01:31:40,797
To die,

2332
01:31:43,933 --> 01:31:46,002
to sleep.

2333
01:31:46,035 --> 01:31:48,570
To sleep,

2334
01:31:48,603 --> 01:31:51,373
perchance to dream.

2335
01:31:52,608 --> 01:31:55,277
Aye, there's the rub!
